# Veripsa release gate (auto-discovered). One gate per file -> two PRs adding gates add two
# FILES, never a shared line -> no false merge conflict on registration. register_gate is
# defined in run_gates.sh. Format: register_gate <test> <marker> <name> <ok_blurb> <desc> [tail]
register_gate "tests/test_stale_branch_reservation_decay.py" "BRANCH-RESERVATION ADVISORY-ONLY GATE: PASS" "BR reservations are advisory-only, never a hard merge-queue block" "branch-reservation ADVISORY-ONLY (#851 ROOT MODEL, gen 8): a push to a non-main branch reserves a lane under change_id 'BR-<branch>' on the main coordinate before any PR exists; by invariant a 'BR-%' claim means NO open PR yet, so it is NOT in the merge queue and must NEVER hard-block a real PR, REGARDLESS OF AGE. This SUPERSEDES the #917/#923 age-threshold decay (which only softened a BR once it was idle past a knob). A read-time classifier in core.main_impact_surface computes holder_advisory = (change_id LIKE 'BR-%' AND NOT core._branch_matches_release over release_branch_patterns) with NO age term: a same-path collision whose HOLDER is a non-release 'BR-' claim downgrades from a blocking 'serialize' to a non-blocking 'serialize_soft' (the neutral heads-up, no pause/ACK) and the advisory holder drops out of serialize_behind. An advisory BR must not stop the waiting PRs from ordering among THEMSELVES, so waits_all adds a synthetic (later-PR -> earliest-PR) HARD wait whenever the active lane holder is an advisory BR: the EARLIEST real PR is the effective leader (heads-up) and later PRs hard-serialize behind that earlier PR, never behind the BR. The gate drives the real make_db_processor against scratch Postgres: (1) PR-BEHIND-BR-ONLY -> LEADER (a FRESH, never-backdated non-release BR + one upstream PR on the same path -> serialize_soft, empty serialize_behind, no action_required / 'Wait in line' / ACK copy; proven with the BR's real-push signal ~now so AGE cannot be the reason -- the old age-decay would have hard-blocked it), (2) PR-vs-PR STILL HARD (the ordinary merge queue is unchanged -- leader/follower serialize), (3) PR-vs-PR BEHIND A BR (the leader is the earliest PR; the later PR's serialize_behind names that PR, never the BR), (4) RELEASE-PATTERN EXEMPTION (a BR matching release_branch_patterns STILL hard-blocks -- the opt-in exemption is preserved), (5) CONTENT-FREE: branch names, repo ids, paths, and timestamps only; a file body token never reaches a rendered check/comment." "a non-release BR-<branch> reservation with no open PR is advisory-only REGARDLESS OF AGE -- it never hard-blocks a real PR (downgraded to a non-blocking heads-up) and the real PRs still order among themselves, while a policy-pinned release-pattern BR and the ordinary PR-vs-PR queue still serialize -- recall preserved (#851 root model)" 18
